          %h3#classical_solutions_and_bps_states Classical solutions and BPS states
          %p
            Bosonic solutions of eleven-dimensional supergravity were studied in the 1980s in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} Kaluza-Klein
            supergravity. The topic received renewed attention in the mid-to-late 1990s as a result of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            and duality paradigm and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
          %p
            One of the earliest solutions of eleven-dimensional supergravity is the maximally supersymmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freund-Rubin+compactification"} Freund-Rubin compactification
            with geometry
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_4 \times S^7
            and 4-form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            proportional to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi AdS
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_4
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freund"}> Peter Freund
              , Mark Rubin,
              %em Dynamics of Dimensional Reduction
              Phys.Lett. B97 (1980) 233-235 (
              %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/154579?ln=en"}> inSpire
              )
          %p
            The radii of curvatures of the two factors are furthermore in a ratio of 1:2. The modern avatar of this solution is as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near-horizon+geometry"} near-horizon geometry
            of coincident
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-branes"} M2-branes
          %ul
            %li#DuffStelle91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Duff"}> Mike Duff
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kellogg+Stelle"} Kellogg Stelle
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Multi-membrane solutions of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                        %mo =
                        %mn 11
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 11
                  supergravity
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/299386"} spire:299386
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(91)91371-2"} doi:10.1016/0370-2693(91)91371-2
          %p
            Shortly after the original Freund-Rubin solution was discovered, Englert discovered a deformation of this solution where one could turn on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
            on the
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^7
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+spinors"} Killing spinors
            of the solution, a suitable multiple of the 4-form one constructs by squaring the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            can be added to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
